      {
        "text": "These findings challenge assumptions underpinning many network-based prevention programs and underscore the critical need for theories and interventions that are culturally and contextually grounded in the lived experiences of AI youth.",
        "page": 1
      },
      {
        "text": "Because the conventional assumption in much of the adolescent health literature, that risky behavior is a primary determinant of relationships in youth, does not hold in our study, which may also mean that common network intervention strategies aimed at reducing substance use may not be as effective as previously thought",
        "page": 16
      },
      {
        "text": "Given that this assumption does not align with our findings, it becomes imperative to investigate culturally relevant alternatives to understanding of how substance use relates to friendship formation.",
